claire: Heads up 1 linkcheck in CI flagged a bunch of anchors after the last content sweep. Mostly pages under /sdk/snippets and a migration guide.

ci-bot: docs/linkcheck job #201 failed  9 broken anchors. Artifact: https://ci.redwood.ai/artifacts/docs/linkcheck/201

todd: I pulled the preview and most broken anchors look like they came from headings containing parentheses, inline code, or emoji. Examples: 
- 'Calling the embed() helper (quickstart)' became id="calling-the-embed()-helper"
- 'SDK: response `id` field' became id="sdk-response-`id`-field"
These generate unhelpful slugs and our internal links use simplified versions so they don't match.

lee: Options: 1) sanitize slugs at build time (strip punctuation, normalize emoji) via a remark/rehype plugin; 2) add explicit HTML ids on the problematic headings; 3) change internal links to the generated slugs (messy).

claire: For the short term let's patch the most visited pages with explicit ids so the previews are useful for customers. For long term we should normalize the slugger so everyone gets consistent ids locally and in CI.

lee: I've drafted a small rehype plugin that normalizes heading ids to kebab-case and removes inline code ticks, parens, and emoji. It also logs collisions and appends incremental suffixes. PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/4360 (draft).

ci-bot: deploy preview created for PR 4360  https://deploy-preview-4360--docs.redwood.ai. Running linkcheck on preview.

todd: I pushed a couple of small doc edits that add explicit anchors to the two sdk/snippets pages causing most 404s: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/4361. That unblocks the product page linking to snippets.

claire: can we also add a lint rule so authors get warned in the editor if they put inline code in headings? The CI linkcheck is too slow for authoring.

lee: yes, the plugin bundle includes a docs-lint rule that flags headings containing inline code or emoji. I added a VSCode tasks snippet in the PR to run the rule locally.

claire: great. steps to reproduce locally for reviewers: 
1) clone the repo
2) yarn
3) yarn workspace docs build (or yarn docs:dev)
4) node ./scripts/run-local-linkcheck.js

lee: sample plugin (simplified) for the PR:

const toHAST = require('mdast-util-to-hast');
module.exports = function normalizeHeadingIds() {
  return (tree, file) => {
    visit(tree, 'heading', (node) => {
      const text = toString(node);
      let slug = text.toLowerCase()
        .replace(/[`()[\]{}\u{1F600}-\u{1F64F}]/gu, '') // strip ticks, parens, square braces, emoji range
        .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-')
        .replace(/(^-|-$)/g, '');
      // collision handling
      let id = slug;
      let i = 1;
      while (usedIds.has(id)) { id = `${slug}-${i++}`; }
      usedIds.add(id);
      if (!node.data) node.data = {};
      if (!node.data.hProperties) node.data.hProperties = {};
      node.data.hProperties.id = id;
    });
  };
};

claire: That looks good. Make sure tests cover non-latin characters and existing explicit ids. Also add a migration note: contributors should avoid relying on automatic slug patterns and pin explicit ids if they need stable anchors across translations.

docs-lint: Added a rule file docs/lint-rules/no-inline-code-heading.js and unit tests.

claire: OK let's merge in this order: 4361 (explicit anchor patches), then 4360 (plugin + lint). After that we'll run a full site build and linkcheck. If the preview still reports issues, we revert 4361 and iterate.

todd: I'll monitor the preview and ping infra if linkcheck flakes.

lee: FYI I added a small note to docs/migrations.md about heading ID changes and a short script contributors can run to list headings and ids: scripts/list-heading-ids.js

claire: thanks everyone. Once merged, I'll send a short note in #docs announcing the change and the recommended authoring pattern (avoid inline code in headings or add explicit ids).

todd: :+1: quick follow-up: we should add the normalization to our mkdocs/docusaurus config so both toolchains behave the same in case of future migrations.

lee: noted. I'll add a small section in the PR about integrating with both static site builders.

claire: merging when linkcheck passes.
